-- Singapore Penal Code 1871 — extortion.
--
--   s 383  defines "extortion"                            (the elements)
--   s 384  punishes whoever commits extortion             (the charge)
--
-- A charge is laid under the PUNISHING section, so this module exports one
-- charge (s 384) over one facts record, and one offence ladder over the s 383
-- elements.
--
-- s 383 is IMPORTed by robbery-390-392.l4, which nests an `Extortion Facts`
-- inside its own record because s 390(1) says "In all robbery there is either
-- theft or extortion", and s 390(3) says when an extortion is a robbery.
--
-- The reported charge this module is pinned to:
--
--   Sarjit Singh Rapati v Public Prosecutor [2005] SGHC 28 — s 384 read with
--   s 34. The charge is quoted in the judgment and reproduced in the directives
--   below, modulo the citation form ("Penal Code, Chapter 224 read with Section
--   34 of the same Act" then, "Penal Code 1871" now) and the CPC s 124 header,
--   which this row normalises across every charge it frames.
--
-- An ISOMORPHIC encoding in INERT STYLE — see culpable-homicide-301.l4 for the
-- conventions and cheating-415-417-420.l4 for the charge machinery. Verbatim
-- statutory prose rides as inert strings; only the operative atoms are lifted
-- into named leaves; the inert strings are mortar, never shadow copies of the
-- leaf beside them.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The text
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- 383. Whoever intentionally puts any person in fear of any harm to that person
--      or to any other person, in body, mind, reputation or property, whether
--      such harm is to be caused legally or illegally, and thereby dishonestly
--      induces the person so put in fear to deliver to any person any property
--      or valuable security, or anything signed or sealed which may be converted
--      into a valuable security, commits "extortion".
--      Illustrations
--      (a) A threatens to publish a defamatory libel concerning Z, unless Z
--          gives him money. He thus induces Z to give him money. A has
--          committed extortion.
--      (b) A threatens Z that he will keep Z's child in wrongful confinement,
--          unless Z will sign and deliver to A a promissory note binding Z to
--          pay certain moneys to A. Z signs and delivers the note. A has
--          committed extortion.
--      (c) A, an enforcement officer, sees Z committing an offence, and
--          threatens to report the offence unless Z gives him money. Z fears
--          that the report may result in his being prosecuted for the offence
--          and delivers money to A. A has committed extortion.
--      (d) A, by putting Z in fear of grievous hurt, dishonestly induces Z to
--          sign or affix his seal to a blank paper and deliver it to A. Z signs
--          and delivers the paper to A. Here, as the paper so signed may be
--          converted into a valuable security, A has committed extortion.
--
-- 384. Whoever commits extortion shall be punished with imprisonment for a term
--      of not less than 2 years and not more than 7 years and with caning.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The shape of the section
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- s 383 is one sentence with a spine of conjoined elements and three
-- disjunctive groups hanging off it:
--
--   THE ACT      intentionally + puts any person in fear
--   WHOSE HARM   that person OR any other person         (Illustration (b): the
--                child of the person threatened; nothing requires the harm to
--                be feared for oneself)
--   WHAT HARM    in body, mind, reputation OR property   (Illustration (a): a
--                defamatory libel is harm in reputation)
--   THE LINK     dishonestly (s 24) + thereby induces the person so put in fear
--   THE DELIVERY any property OR valuable security OR anything signed or sealed
--                which may be converted into one         (Illustration (d))
--
-- "whether such harm is to be caused legally or illegally" removes a defence
-- rather than adding an element, so it rides inert — and Illustration (c) is
-- what it is there for: an enforcement officer who threatens to do the very
-- thing he is entitled to do still puts Z in fear within the section. Note what
-- that leaves carrying the whole weight. Once a lawful threat is inside s 383,
-- `dishonestly` is the ONLY leaf standing between this section and ordinary
-- debt collection, which is exactly what the negative fixture below turns on.
--
-- Two calls this encoding makes, recorded rather than hidden:
--
--   1. `induced the person so put in fear` is its own leaf, separate from the
--      delivery group. "Thereby … induces" is a causal claim: a person who is
--      in fear and who hands something over for an unrelated reason has not
--      been extorted, and there is nowhere else on the ladder to fail that.
--   2. `that person` and `any other person` are kept as two leaves rather than
--      one "whose harm" question. They are not the same fact, and the second is
--      the one Illustration (b) and s 390(3) both turn on — an encoding that
--      collapsed them could not express the difference between Illustration (b)
--      and Illustration (c) of s 390.
--
-- The particulars the charge recites but the section does not test (who the
-- victim is, what was extorted, what the threat was) are STRING fields on the
-- same record, so one record is both the ladder's input and the charge's.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 125 and the recital: extortion MUST state the manner
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- CPC 2010 s 125 illustration (b) is stated for cheating, but the reason it
-- gives reaches extortion on the same footing: without the manner, "extortion"
-- alone does not tell the accused what he is said to have done. The reported
-- Sarjit Singh charge does state it, and that is the form this module builds:
--
--   did extort <the property extorted> from <one victim> by intentionally
--   putting <object pronoun> in fear <the fear>
--
-- Contrast theft-378-379.l4 and robbery-390-392.l4, where s 125 illustration
-- (a) says the manner need NOT be stated and the recital stops at the thing and
-- the person. The recital depth in this row is section-dependent, and this is
-- the section at the deep end.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `penal-code-general`

§ `Penal Code 1871 — extortion`

§§ `The facts of one threat`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 383 — the operative atoms, and the particulars CPC ss 124 and 125 require
DECLARE `Extortion Facts` HAS
    particulars             IS A Particulars @desc Who is charged, and when and where the offence was committed
    victim                  IS A STRING  @desc The name of the person put in fear, as it is to appear in the charge — for example "Mohammad Sharful Islam [“Sharful”]"
    `victim described as`   IS A STRING  @desc How the charge describes that person, after the name — for example "M/27 yrs"; leave blank if not needed
    `victim pronoun`        IS A Pronoun @desc The pronoun the charge uses for the person put in fear
    `the property extorted` IS A STRING  @desc What was delivered up, as it is to read after "did extort" — for example "cash of $200/-"
    `the fear`              IS A STRING  @desc What the victim was put in fear of, as it is to read after "in fear" — for example "that you would continue to keep his cousin, one Md Faruq Ahmed [“Faruq”], M/23 yrs, in wrongful confinement"

    -- "intentionally puts any person in fear"
    intentionally           IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused mean to put the victim in fear, as opposed to frightening them by accident or in passing?
    `put the victim in fear` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the victim in fact put in fear? A threat that the victim shrugs off has not put them in fear.

    -- "of any harm to that person or to any other person"
    `that person`           IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the harm feared harm to the victim themselves?
    `any other person`      IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the harm feared harm to somebody other than the victim? Illustration (b): the threat was to keep Z's child in wrongful confinement, and the fear was Z's.

    -- "in body, mind, reputation or property"
    body                    IS A BOOLEAN @desc … harm in body — death, hurt, confinement, or any other harm to the person?
    mind                    IS A BOOLEAN @desc … harm in mind?
    reputation              IS A BOOLEAN @desc … harm in reputation? Illustration (a): a threat to publish a defamatory libel.
    property                IS A BOOLEAN @desc … harm in property?

    -- "whether such harm is to be caused legally or illegally, and thereby
    --  dishonestly induces the person so put in fear to deliver"
    dishonestly             IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused act dishonestly — intending a wrongful gain or a wrongful loss, or in a way reasonable and honest people would call dishonest, knowing that (s 24)? It does not matter that the threatened harm was one the accused was entitled to cause; a creditor who frightens a debtor into paying what is actually owed gains nothing wrongfully.
    `induced the person so put in fear`
                            IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the delivery brought about BY that fear? Somebody who is in fear but who hands the thing over for an unrelated reason has not been extorted.

    -- "to any person any property or valuable security, or anything signed or
    --  sealed which may be converted into a valuable security"
    `any property`          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was property delivered — money or property of any description (s 22)?
    `valuable security`     IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a valuable security delivered?
    `anything signed or sealed which may be converted into a valuable security`
                            IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was something signed or sealed delivered which may be turned into a valuable security? Illustration (d): a blank paper the victim was made to sign.

§§ `s 383 — extortion`

@export Whether the accused "commits extortion" within section 383 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS AN `Extortion Facts` @desc The threat, whom it frightened, and what it got
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `commits extortion` f IF
        "Whoever"
   ...  f's intentionally
   ...  f's `put the victim in fear`
   ...  "of any harm to"
   ...  (    f's `that person`
        OR   f's `any other person` )
   ...  ", in"
   ...  (    f's body
        ..   f's mind
        ..   f's reputation
        OR   f's property )
   ...  ", whether such harm is to be caused legally or illegally, and thereby"
   ...  f's dishonestly
   ...  f's `induced the person so put in fear`
   ...  "to deliver to any person"
   ...  (    f's `any property`
        ..   f's `valuable security`
        OR   f's `anything signed or sealed which may be converted into a valuable security` )
   ...  ", commits “extortion”"

§§ `s 384 — punishment for extortion`

@export Whether the accused has committed an offence punishable under section 384 of the Penal Code 1871 (extortion)
GIVEN f IS AN `Extortion Facts` @desc The threat, whom it frightened, and what it got
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `offence under s 384` f IF
        "Whoever"
   ...  `commits extortion` f

§§ `The charge`

-- The manner clause s 125 requires. Sarjit Singh Rapati:
--
--   did extort cash of $200/- from one Mohammad Sharful Islam ["Sharful"],
--   M/27 yrs by intentionally putting him in fear that you would continue to
--   keep his cousin, one Md Faruq Ahmed ["Faruq"], M/23 yrs, in wrongful
--   confinement
GIVEN f IS AN `Extortion Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`the manner of the extortion` f MEANS
    CONCAT "did extort ", f's `the property extorted`,
           " from ", (`one` (f's victim) (f's `victim described as`)),
           " by intentionally putting ", (`object pronoun` (f's `victim pronoun`)),
           " in fear ", f's `the fear`

-- The elements, named for the refusal. Every leaf and every disjunctive group
-- of s 383 is named here, so — unlike the cheating module — no "the elements
-- taken together" row is needed: nothing can fail the ladder without one of
-- these rows firing.
GIVEN f IS AN `Extortion Facts`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`elements of extortion not made out` f MEANS
    concat (LIST
        (`missing unless` (f's intentionally) "intentionally"),
        (`missing unless` (f's `put the victim in fear`) "puts any person in fear"),
        (`missing unless` (f's `that person` OR f's `any other person`) "of any harm to that person or to any other person"),
        (`missing unless` (f's body OR f's mind OR f's reputation OR f's property) "in body, mind, reputation or property"),
        (`missing unless` (f's dishonestly) "dishonestly (section 24)"),
        (`missing unless` (f's `induced the person so put in fear`) "thereby induces the person so put in fear"),
        (`missing unless` (f's `any property` OR f's `valuable security` OR f's `anything signed or sealed which may be converted into a valuable security`) "to deliver any property or valuable security, or anything signed or sealed which may be converted into a valuable security"))

@export Frame a charge under section 384 of the Penal Code 1871 (extortion), or say why none can be framed
GIVEN f IS AN `Extortion Facts` @desc The threat, what it got, and the particulars of the charge
GIVETH A Charge
`charge under s 384` f MEANS
    `frame the charge` (f's particulars) "384" "extortion"
        "imprisonment for a term of not less than 2 years and not more than 7 years and with caning"
        (`offence under s 384` f)
        (`the manner of the extortion` f)
        (`elements of extortion not made out` f)

§§ `Sarjit Singh Rapati v Public Prosecutor [2005] SGHC 28`

-- The appellant and a co-accused held one Md Faruq Ahmed, and obtained $200
-- from Faruq's cousin Sharful by putting Sharful in fear that Faruq would be
-- kept in wrongful confinement. The charge, as quoted in the judgment:
--
--   … did extort cash of $200/- from one Mohammad Sharful Islam ["Sharful"],
--   M/27 yrs by intentionally putting him in fear that you would continue to
--   keep his cousin, one Md Faruq Ahmed ["Faruq"], M/23 yrs, in wrongful
--   confinement and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under
--   Section 384 of the Penal Code, Chapter 224 read with Section 34 of the
--   same Act.
--
-- The particulars are the ones penal-code-general.l4 already carries as
-- `Sarjit Singh particulars`, and are reused rather than restated.
--
-- Two interpretation calls, both recorded because the judgment leaves them
-- open. The harm feared was harm to ANOTHER person — the cousin, not Sharful —
-- so `that person` is FALSE and `any other person` is TRUE; that is what the
-- charge says on its face. And the harm is classified as harm in BODY, on the
-- footing that continued wrongful confinement is a restraint of Faruq's person:
-- the judgment does not classify it, and "mind" is arguable on the same facts,
-- so a reviewer should look here first.
`Sarjit Singh Rapati` MEANS `Extortion Facts` WITH
    particulars             IS `Sarjit Singh particulars`
    victim                  IS "Mohammad Sharful Islam [“Sharful”]"
    `victim described as`   IS "M/27 yrs"
    `victim pronoun`        IS he
    `the property extorted` IS "cash of $200/-"
    `the fear`              IS "that you would continue to keep his cousin, one Md Faruq Ahmed [“Faruq”], M/23 yrs, in wrongful confinement"
    intentionally           IS TRUE
    `put the victim in fear` IS TRUE
    `that person`           IS FALSE
    `any other person`      IS TRUE
    body                    IS TRUE
    mind                    IS FALSE
    reputation              IS FALSE
    property                IS FALSE
    dishonestly             IS TRUE
    `induced the person so put in fear` IS TRUE
    `any property`          IS TRUE
    `valuable security`     IS FALSE
    `anything signed or sealed which may be converted into a valuable security` IS FALSE

-- The same shape of facts with the dishonesty taken out: a creditor who
-- frightens someone into paying a debt actually owed. Every other leaf stands —
-- the fear is real, and "whether such harm is to be caused legally or
-- illegally" means a lawful threat does not save him either — but there is no
-- wrongful gain and no wrongful loss, so the inducement is not dishonest and
-- s 383 fails on that one leaf. This is the fixture that shows how much weight
-- `dishonestly` is carrying in this section.
`Sarjit Singh Rapati, no dishonest inducement` MEANS `Extortion Facts` WITH
    particulars             IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s particulars
    victim                  IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s victim
    `victim described as`   IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s `victim described as`
    `victim pronoun`        IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s `victim pronoun`
    `the property extorted` IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s `the property extorted`
    `the fear`              IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s `the fear`
    intentionally           IS TRUE
    `put the victim in fear` IS TRUE
    `that person`           IS FALSE
    `any other person`      IS TRUE
    body                    IS TRUE
    mind                    IS FALSE
    reputation              IS FALSE
    property                IS FALSE
    dishonestly             IS FALSE
    `induced the person so put in fear` IS TRUE
    `any property`          IS TRUE
    `valuable security`     IS FALSE
    `anything signed or sealed which may be converted into a valuable security` IS FALSE

-- A threat that got nothing: the victim was put in fear, dishonestly, and
-- handed nothing over. s 383 is not an inchoate offence — the delivery is an
-- element — so the whole delivery group fails and with it the causal leaf that
-- depends on there having been a delivery to induce.
`Sarjit Singh Rapati, nothing delivered` MEANS `Extortion Facts` WITH
    particulars             IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s particulars
    victim                  IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s victim
    `victim described as`   IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s `victim described as`
    `victim pronoun`        IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s `victim pronoun`
    `the property extorted` IS ""
    `the fear`              IS `Sarjit Singh Rapati`'s `the fear`
    intentionally           IS TRUE
    `put the victim in fear` IS TRUE
    `that person`           IS FALSE
    `any other person`      IS TRUE
    body                    IS TRUE
    mind                    IS FALSE
    reputation              IS FALSE
    property                IS FALSE
    dishonestly             IS TRUE
    `induced the person so put in fear` IS FALSE
    `any property`          IS FALSE
    `valuable security`     IS FALSE
    `anything signed or sealed which may be converted into a valuable security` IS FALSE

§§ `Directives`

#EVAL `commits extortion` `Sarjit Singh Rapati`
#EVAL (`charge under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati`)'s text
#EVAL (`charge under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati, no dishonest inducement`)'s refusal
#EVAL (`charge under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati, nothing delivered`)'s refusal

-- The reported s 384 charge, verbatim from the judgment save for the citation
-- form ("Penal Code, Chapter 224 read with Section 34 of the same Act" →
-- "Penal Code 1871") and the normalised CPC s 124 header.
#ASSERT `commits extortion` `Sarjit Singh Rapati`
#ASSERT `offence under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati`
#ASSERT (`charge under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati`)'s `made out`
#ASSERT `the manner of the extortion` `Sarjit Singh Rapati` EQUALS "did extort cash of $200/- from one Mohammad Sharful Islam [“Sharful”], M/27 yrs by intentionally putting him in fear that you would continue to keep his cousin, one Md Faruq Ahmed [“Faruq”], M/23 yrs, in wrongful confinement"
#ASSERT (`charge under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati`)'s text EQUALS "You, Sarjit Singh Rapati, are charged that you, on or about 10 July 2003, at about 12.30 pm, at Newton Hawker Centre car-park, Singapore, together with one Paramjit Singh s/o Buta Singh (M/43 yrs, NRIC No. S1436488-I) and in furtherance of the common intention of you all, did extort cash of $200/- from one Mohammad Sharful Islam [“Sharful”], M/27 yrs by intentionally putting him in fear that you would continue to keep his cousin, one Md Faruq Ahmed [“Faruq”], M/23 yrs, in wrongful confinement, and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section 384 read with section 34 of the Penal Code 1871."
#ASSERT (`charge under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati`)'s punishment EQUALS "imprisonment for a term of not less than 2 years and not more than 7 years and with caning"

-- No dishonest inducement: one leaf off, no charge, and the refusal names it.
#ASSERT NOT (`commits extortion` `Sarjit Singh Rapati, no dishonest inducement`)
#ASSERT NOT ((`charge under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati, no dishonest inducement`)'s `made out`)
#ASSERT (`charge under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati, no dishonest inducement`)'s text EQUALS ""
#ASSERT (`charge under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati, no dishonest inducement`)'s refusal EQUALS "A charge under section 384 would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of extortion (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: dishonestly (section 24)."

-- Nothing delivered: the causal leaf and the whole delivery group fall.
#ASSERT NOT (`commits extortion` `Sarjit Singh Rapati, nothing delivered`)
#ASSERT (`charge under s 384` `Sarjit Singh Rapati, nothing delivered`)'s refusal EQUALS "A charge under section 384 would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of extortion (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: thereby induces the person so put in fear; to deliver any property or valuable security, or anything signed or sealed which may be converted into a valuable security."
